o  end the conflict it's allowing voluntary  departure of some of its staff and their  family members in the capital because of  escalating violence  washington has sent its special envoy  for the horn of africa to ethiopia and  there are also regional efforts to  de-escalate the ugandan president has  called a meeting of the east african  block to discuss the crisis  for now addis ababa remains on edge as  neither side shows any sign of backing  down  priyanka gupta al-jazeera  okay let's bring in independent  journalist samuel getachow in addis  ababa hello there samuel how are  ethiopians reacting to these latest  developments  ethiopians are really concerned the u.s  the biden administration envoy is in  town  saying that there has to be some kind of  negotiation  to end this conflict that began a year  ago it was echoed by the president of  uganda the president of kenya putting so  much pressure to the ethiopian  government as well as the tpnf to  somehow find a solution to a conflict  that started a year ago and has produced  millions of victims from all sides it's  not just in tegra it's also in amhara  and afar as reflected by the un report  that came out yesterday with the support  of the ethiopian human rights  the victims are just so overwhelming and  mounting by that  each day and what has really highlighted  been highlighted about this conflict is  the sexual violence that has been used  by all actors according to the report  so just how critical will this meeting  be which will be held by the east  african block later this month  that will be on november 16th uh the  ugandan president is seen as an elder uh  and a younger generation around  governments all over in the horn of  africa um and it's going to be one of  the options to bring this to a  conclusion because once again the u.n is  saying uh there's going to be some sort  of famine in ethiopia coming back to an  old era and there's also foreign  investment and the issue of foreigners  fleeing eth